# What packwiz indexes (ADR-0024).
#
# The pack root is the repo root, so `packwiz refresh` would otherwise hash every
# tracked file — giving documentation, tests and build scripts a second, hash-based
# source of truth alongside git, and dirtying the manifest on every prose edit.
#
# The index covers pack *content* only: mods/, config/, kubejs/, defaultconfigs/,
# data/, packs/. Everything below is excluded. Syntax is gitignore's.

# --- Repo machinery, not pack content -----------------------------------------
docs/
tests/
scripts/
.claude/
.github/
.vscode/
*.md
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# --- The first-party mod's source and build tooling ---------------------------
# `mod/` is fully tracked in git, so hashing it records nothing new.
mod/
gradle/
gradlew
gradlew.bat
gradle.properties
settings.gradle
build.gradle
.gradle/
build/

# --- The jars themselves ------------------------------------------------------
# packwiz assumes `mods/` holds metafiles and that an installer fetches the jars.
# This pack root *is* the playable instance, so the jars are present too — and
# `refresh` would otherwise index each managed mod twice, once as its metafile
# and once as a raw hashed jar (465 entries becomes 586). A mod with a metafile
# is fully described by it; the jar is that metafile's payload, not content in
# its own right.
mods/*.jar

# ...except the two forks, which have no metafile because they exist on no public
# index. Their hash in index.toml is the only record of which build is installed.
!mods/gcyr-*.jar
!mods/Respoiled-*.jar

# `planetaryfactory_core` stays excluded even though it is also local (ADR-0024):
# `installToPack` rebuilds it into mods/ on every `./gradlew build` and Gradle
# jars are not byte-reproducible, so indexing it would dirty index.toml
# constantly. Its provenance is `mod/`, which git already tracks in full. It is
# covered by the `mods/*.jar` rule above and deliberately not re-included.

# --- Per-user and per-run state, never pack behaviour -------------------------
# NOTE: the repo root is also the live game instance, so it accumulates directories
# that never appear in a fresh checkout. This list was first written from a clean
# worktree and missed every one of them — .probe/ and .mixin.out/ alone added ~1500
# spurious index entries the first time the check ran on a real instance. When adding
# to it, check against `ls -A` in the instance, not against a checkout.
#
# Anchor root-only names with a leading slash. A bare `assets/` matches a directory of
